    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kyoto began in 794 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a> moved the court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>, a grid city modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and it remained the capital until the court moved to Tokyo in 1869.</li><li>Military rulers governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and Edo and, in the Muromachi period, from Kyoto itself, but the city stayed the seat of the emperors, and it was rebuilt after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> of 1467–1477.</li><li>After the capital moved Kyoto built a canal, electric power and a modern municipality, and it was removed from the list of atomic bomb targets, so that much of its older building survives.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first electric tram line ran in Kyoto, made possible by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a> Canal, which supplied hydroelectric power from 1895.</p></aside>
    </section>

